If one IP can hit your API 1000 times before you react, the limit is too loose.

  Best practice is layered:

  1. Don’t rely on IP only.
     Use at least:

  - per IP
  - per account / username / email
  - per API key / session
  - per endpoint

  IP-only blocking is weak because of NAT, VPNs, proxies, and distributed attacks.

  2. Treat login/auth endpoints much more strictly than normal API calls.
     Good starting points:

  - /login, /auth, /forgot-password, OTP verify:
      - 5 to 10 attempts per account per 10 to 15 minutes
      - 20 to 30 attempts per IP per 5 to 10 minutes
      - then add delay, CAPTCHA/challenge, or temporary lock
  - normal authenticated API:
      - rate-limit by API key/user and IP
      - example starting point: 5 r/s with a small burst for ordinary reads
      - much lower for expensive endpoints

  3. Use progressive controls, not just a hard ban.
     Recommended flow:

  - small excess: return 429
  - repeated abuse: add delay/backoff
  - continued abuse: temporary block for 15 to 60 minutes
  - extreme abuse: WAF/CDN firewall rule or denylist

  4. Make brute force harder at the app layer.
     For auth endpoints:

  - generic error messages
  - MFA for admins/sensitive users
  - password hashing done correctly
  - no username enumeration
  - device/risk checks if possible

  5. Log and alert aggressively.
     Alert on:

  - many failures from one IP
  - one account attacked from many IPs
  - many usernames from one IP
  - spikes on auth endpoints
  - 429 surge

  6. Limit concurrency too, not only request rate.
     For abuse and DoS protection, use both:

  - request rate limiting
  - connection limiting

  For nginx, that means:

  - limit_req for request rate
  - limit_conn for concurrent requests/connections

  7. Put this in front of nginx too if possible.
     Best stack:

  - CDN/WAF first
  - nginx rate limiting second
  - app-level account throttling third

  So the practical answer to your example is:

  - do not wait for 1000 requests from one IP
  - for login endpoints, start throttling after single-digit or low double-digit failures
  - for general API, start rate limiting much earlier and return 429
  - combine IP + account + endpoint limits

  For your nginx-based setup, my recommendation would be:

  - limit_req on /api/method/login, OTP, password reset, token endpoints
  - stricter zone for auth endpoints
  - broader but looser zone for normal API
  - limit_conn for abusive concurrency
  - app-side failed-login counter per account

  Sources:

  - OWASP brute force guidance: https://owasp.org/index.php/Blocking_Brute_Force_Attacks
  - OWASP API rate limiting risk: https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2019/en/0xa4-lack-of-resources-and-rate-limiting/
  - NIST on throttling as primary defense: https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3-Implementation-Resources/63B/Authenticators/
  - NGINX limit_req: https://nginx.org/r/limit_req_zone
  - NGINX limit_conn: https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_limit_conn_module.html

